@page "/hero"
@layout BareLayout
@inject IJSRuntime JS
@inject LocaleState LocaleState
@using System.Globalization
@using System.Reflection
@using JustDummies

@*
    The landing page's live hero (§9.8), which the site embeds once the visitor asks for it.

    WHAT IS EDITABLE, AND WHY THAT MUCH. The chain itself is compiled: the links are fixed,
    their arguments are not. §9.8 asks for a pre-filled expression the visitor can modify,
    and the modification that carries the demonstration is the one that changes what must be
    true of the value — a prefix, and the range its length must fall in.

    Changing *which* links are in the chain is the playground's job, and it needs what §10.4
    decides: a catalogue generated at build time from the assembly's metadata, and a parser
    over it. Neither exists yet. Faking it with a hand-written registry is the option §10.4
    rejects by name, so this page does not: it runs one real chain and lets its arguments
    move.

    WHAT MUST NOT BE NEUTRALISED (§9.9). Raise the minimum above the maximum, or shrink the
    maximum below what the prefix alone already requires, and the library refuses the
    declaration. That refusal is the demonstration defending itself, and it is displayed as
    the library worded it. Catching it to keep the home page tidy would remove the best
    argument on the page.

    THE TWO KINDS OF STOP. A range the prefix cannot fit inside, or a minimum past the
    maximum, is the library's own refusal, in Draw() below, and stays in its own words,
    untranslated (§6.4) — it names a JustDummies API contract, not this page's content, and
    it only ever appears at Generate, the moment the chain is declared. Typing past what this
    playground itself allows — 64 for either bound, 10 in the prefix — is this page's own
    rule instead: it is enforced and explained the instant it is hit, in each field's setter
    below, because by the time Generate is next pressed the value has already been silently
    clamped and there would be nothing left to refuse.
*@

@*
    Both classes, and both are load-bearing. `.code-widget` is the card this shares with the
    playground — the box, its padding, the result bar under it, the row of controls under that,
    and the button in that row; `.hero-widget` is what only this one needs, which is now down to
    the depth its continuation lines indent to and the cap hint below the card. They were one
    class until the playground was rebuilt around the same card: see app.css's own header on
    `.code-widget` for why the split is what keeps the two from drifting.
*@
<div class="hero-widget code-widget">
    @*
        Same two-zone shape as the site's own static sample (LiveHero.astro's .sample: a
        <pre> then a <figcaption>, on the same tokens) — this is the card the visitor was
        already looking at, still there, now editable. Matching it isn't decoration: a
        visitor who clicked "Run" and got a different-looking box just watched the page
        replace itself rather than come alive.
    *@
    <div class="card">
        <div class="expression">
            <span><span class="tok-keyword">string</span> reference = <span class="tok-type">Any</span>.<span class="tok-member">String</span>()</span>
            @*
                Both fixed, like String() and Generate() around them, and for two reasons the
                editable links do not share. First, together they are exactly what keeps the
                filler characters safe to display — letters and digits only, upper-case, never
                a control character or a line break through the middle of the result bar.
                Second, and the reason neither one is editable on its own: this is the pair
                Act I explains right below the hero (Snippets/FactoriesConstrained.cs,
                Snippets/Why.cs), and the two have to stay one chain with it — a visitor
                presses "Run" to see the code they are about to read come alive, not a
                different expression they then have to reconcile with what Act I shows.
            *@
            <span>.<span class="tok-member">AlphaNumeric</span>()</span>
            <span>.<span class="tok-member">InUpperCase</span>()</span>
            <span>.<span class="tok-member">StartingWith</span>(<span class="tok-string">"</span><input type="text" class="tok-string" data-width="@FieldWidth(_prefix)" @bind:get="_prefix" @bind:set="SetPrefix" @bind:event="oninput" aria-label='@PlaygroundStrings.T(LocaleState.Current, "hero.aria.prefix")' aria-describedby="@(_capHintOwner == "prefix" ? "cap-hint" : null)" /><span class="tok-string">"</span>)</span>
            <span>.<span class="tok-member">WithLengthBetween</span>(<input type="number" class="tok-number" data-width="@FieldWidth(_minText)" @bind:get="_minText" @bind:set="SetMinText" @bind:event="oninput" aria-label='@PlaygroundStrings.T(LocaleState.Current, "hero.aria.min")' aria-describedby="@(_capHintOwner == "min" ? "cap-hint" : null)" min="0" max="@MaxLength" />, <input type="number" class="tok-number" data-width="@FieldWidth(_maxText)" @bind:get="_maxText" @bind:set="SetMaxText" @bind:event="oninput" aria-label='@PlaygroundStrings.T(LocaleState.Current, "hero.aria.max")' aria-describedby="@(_capHintOwner == "max" ? "cap-hint" : null)" min="0" max="@MaxLength" />)</span>
            <span>.<span class="tok-member">Generate</span>();</span>
        </div>

        @*
            One shape for both outcomes: the arrow, the label, then what came back — a value in
            the generated colour, or the library's refusal in the error one. The refusal used to
            get neither the arrow nor the label, on the reasoning that "→" promises a value and a
            refusal is not one. What that actually bought was a bar that changed shape under the
            reader between two presses of the same button, and the colour already says which of
            the two they are looking at.
        *@
        <div class="result-bar">
            <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
            <span class="label">@PlaygroundStrings.T(LocaleState.Current, "hero.produced")</span>
            @if (_refusal is null) {
                <output class="value">@_value</output>
            } else {
                <output class="refusal">@_refusal</output>
            }
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="controls">
        <button class="generate" @onclick="Draw" disabled="@(_min is null || _max is null)">@PlaygroundStrings.T(LocaleState.Current, "hero.generate")</button>
        <span class="live">@PlaygroundStrings.T(LocaleState.Current, "hero.running", LibraryVersion)</span>
    </div>

    @*
        Always in the document, never inserted only once there is something to say: a live
        region a screen reader has not seen yet is a live region it has no reason to announce
        the first change to. Empty, it takes no visible space and every input already names it
        through aria-describedby, so it says nothing extra until a cap is actually hit.
    *@
    <p id="cap-hint" class="hint" role="status">@_capHint</p>
</div>

@code {

    /// <summary>What this playground itself allows in <c>.StartingWith(...)</c>.</summary>
    private const int TextLimit = 10;

    /// <summary>What this playground itself allows for either bound of <c>.WithLengthBetween(...)</c> — the library has no such limit.</summary>
    private const int MaxLength = 64;

    private string _prefix  = "ORD-";
    private string _minText = "8";
    private string _maxText = "20";

    /// <summary>
    ///     The bounds Generate actually runs with, or <c>null</c> while the field showing one
    ///     does not currently hold a number — an empty field mid-edit, or an incomplete token
    ///     such as <c>"1e"</c>. Kept separate from <see cref="_minText" />/<see cref="_maxText" />
    ///     so a value that cannot be used is never silently replaced by the last one that could:
    ///     without this, clearing a field and pressing Generate would draw against the
    ///     *previous* bound while the expression on screen visibly reads
    ///     <c>.WithLengthBetween()</c>.
    /// </summary>
    private int? _min = 8;
    private int? _max = 20;

    private string  _value   = string.Empty;
    private string? _refusal;

    /// <summary>
    ///     Set the instant a field's setter clamps or truncates a value, cleared once the field
    ///     that set it — <see cref="_capHintOwner" /> — is edited back within bounds. A
    ///     transient "you just hit a wall" cue, independent of <see cref="_refusal" />: this is
    ///     the playground's own rule, never the library's, and it is live rather than waiting
    ///     for Generate (see the comment at the top of this file for why it cannot wait).
    /// </summary>
    private string? _capHint;

    /// <summary>
    ///     Which field <see cref="_capHint" /> is currently about — <c>"prefix"</c>,
    ///     <c>"min"</c> or <c>"max"</c> — so only that field's <c>aria-describedby</c> points
    ///     at it. All three inputs pointing at one shared hint meant tabbing from an
    ///     over-length number straight to an untouched text field announced a 64-character
    ///     cap on a field that actually caps at 10.
    /// </summary>
    private string? _capHintOwner;

    private static string LibraryVersion { get; } = ReadLibraryVersion();

    protected override void OnInitialized() {
        Draw();
    }

    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender) {
        // The frame around this page cannot know how tall it is; it is told. Reported after
        // every render because editing an argument can wrap a line or swap the value for a
        // refusal, and a frame that only measured once would clip the very message the
        // scene exists to show.
        await JS.InvokeVoidAsync("jdHeroReportHeight");
    }

    /// <summary>
    ///     Declares the chain, then draws from it.
    ///
    ///     The two are one call here, deliberately: the library refuses a contradictory
    ///     declaration when it is declared, not when it is drawn, and the whole point of the
    ///     scene is that the refusal arrives at that moment.
    ///
    ///     Nothing here checks this playground's own caps: by construction, <see cref="_prefix" />
    ///     can never be longer than <see cref="TextLimit" />, and <see cref="_min" />/<see cref="_max" />
    ///     can never exceed <see cref="MaxLength" /> — see the setters below, which is where
    ///     that limit is actually enforced and explained.
    /// </summary>
    private void Draw() {
        if (_min is null || _max is null) {
            // The Generate button is disabled for exactly this state (see the markup above),
            // so a pointer or keyboard press cannot reach here — this guard is for whatever
            // reaches Draw() some other way, now or later, rather than assume the button is the
            // only caller and call .WithLengthBetween(...) on a value that was never confirmed.
            return;
        }

        try {
            _value = Any.String()
                        .AlphaNumeric()
                        .InUpperCase()
                        .StartingWith(_prefix)
                        .WithLengthBetween(_min.Value, _max.Value)
                        .Generate();

            _refusal = null;
        } catch (Exception refused) when (refused is DummyException or ArgumentException) {
            // DummyException is the base of the library's own family, and catching it rather
            // than a list of framework types is the whole difference between this scene
            // working and not.
            //
            // The first version caught ArgumentException and InvalidOperationException, which
            // is what a contradiction *looks* like it should throw. The library throws
            // ConflictingAnyConstraintException, so the refusal escaped, Blazor's renderer
            // took it as an unhandled error, and the widget showed nothing at all — the
            // refusal neutralised by accident, which is exactly what §9.9 forbids. Found by
            // shortening the length in a browser rather than by reading the code.
            //
            // ArgumentException stays for the guards in front of the constraint layer, such
            // as a minimum above the maximum.
            //
            // Displayed as the library worded it. A message rewritten here would be this
            // page's opinion of the library's diagnostics rather than the diagnostics.
            _refusal = refused.Message;
            _value   = string.Empty;
        }
    }

    private Task SetPrefix(string value) {
        _prefix = Cap(value, TextLimit, "prefix");

        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    /// <summary>
    ///     How wide an editable field renders, in <c>ch</c> — read by app.css off the
    ///     <c>data-width</c> attribute this sets, never inline: the site's CSP is
    ///     <c>style-src 'self'</c> with no <c>unsafe-inline</c>, so a computed
    ///     <c>style="width: …"</c> is silently dropped by the browser rather than applied.
    ///
    ///     The width itself is the field's own content plus one character of room for the
    ///     caret, so typing at the end never sits flush against the dashed underline.
    ///     Without this, every field held the width its widest legal value could ever need,
    ///     so "ORD-" sat in a box built for ten characters, and the quotes drawn either side
    ///     of it (the string fields' own markup) landed nowhere near the text they were
    ///     supposed to wrap.
    ///
    ///     Counted by text element, the same way <see cref="Cap" /> counts what it
    ///     truncates: a raw <see cref="string" /> length would size the box in UTF-16 code
    ///     units, one short of the truth for anything outside the basic plane. Clamped to
    ///     app.css's own <c>[data-width]</c> rules — one per column from 1 to 12, the widest
    ///     a field is ever styled for — so a value <see cref="Cap" /> has not yet caught (a
    ///     paste mid-keystroke) never asks for a rule that does not exist.
    /// </summary>
    private static int FieldWidth(string value) => Math.Clamp(new StringInfo(value).LengthInTextElements + 1, 1, 12);

    /// <summary>
    ///     Used by <see cref="SetPrefix" />: strips <c>"</c> and <c>\</c>, then truncates what
    ///     is left to <paramref name="limit" /> characters, setting <see cref="_capHint" /> the
    ///     moment a keystroke or a paste hits either rule.
    ///
    ///     THE STRIPPING IS WHAT KEEPS THE EXPRESSION SHOWN REAL. Both characters are drawn
    ///     literally, either side of the input, as the C# string-literal quotes (Hero.razor's
    ///     markup) — a quote or a backslash typed into the field would need escaping to stay
    ///     valid there, and this widget draws the value as typed, not re-serialised. A field
    ///     holding <c>a"b</c> would otherwise be shown as <c>StartingWith("a"b")</c>: no longer
    ///     one string literal, and no longer the expression <see cref="Draw" /> actually runs
    ///     — it still passes the library the raw value and still draws correctly, so the
    ///     display would be the only thing lying. Stripped rather than escaped, matching how
    ///     the length limit already works: a widget that showed <c>\"</c> for a typed <c>"</c>
    ///     would be showing a different value than the one <see cref="Draw" /> uses, which is
    ///     the same defect from the other side.
    ///
    ///     Deliberately not paired with <c>maxlength</c> on the input, unlike a first version
    ///     of this method was. The browser enforces <c>maxlength</c> before the value ever
    ///     reaches Blazor's <c>oninput</c>, so this method never saw more than the limit and
    ///     never had anything to explain — the truncation worked, silently, which is exactly
    ///     the defect this method exists to fix. This is the sole enforcement now, the same
    ///     way <see cref="ParseAndClamp" /> already is the sole enforcement for the number
    ///     fields.
    ///
    ///     Counted and sliced by text element, not by raw <see cref="string" /> index. A
    ///     supplementary-plane character — most emoji — is two UTF-16 code units in .NET, and a
    ///     limit landing between them would keep only the lead surrogate: not "one character
    ///     short of ten," but a string no longer valid UTF-16 at all, on a field a visitor can
    ///     paste anything into.
    ///
    ///     KEPT TO PRINTABLE ASCII FOR THE SAME REASON AS THE QUOTE STRIP ABOVE — NOT BECAUSE
    ///     THE LIBRARY WOULD REFUSE IT. A literal a caller writes is exempt from every
    ///     character constraint the chain declares (ADR-0079): <c>StartingWith("café-")</c>
    ///     draws exactly that under <c>AlphaNumeric().InUpperCase()</c>, filler included, no
    ///     refusal — there is no library complaint here for this widget to defer to. What a
    ///     control character would still break is this page's own display in the result bar:
    ///     a raw NUL or line break rendered there is this widget failing, not a lesson about
    ///     the library. So it stays this playground's own rule, enforced and explained here
    ///     the instant it is hit, the same shape as the length cap and the quote strip above.
    /// </summary>
    private string Cap(string value, int limit, string owner) {
        string sanitized = value.Replace("\"", string.Empty).Replace("\\", string.Empty);

        // Before the length cap, so what is counted is what survives.
        string printable = new(sanitized.Where(character => character is >= ' ' and <= '~').ToArray());
        StringInfo info = new(printable);

        if (info.LengthInTextElements > limit) {
            SetHint(owner, PlaygroundStrings.T(LocaleState.Current, "limit.text", limit));

            return info.SubstringByTextElements(0, limit);
        }

        // Quotes named first when a keystroke hit both rules: that one is about the expression
        // on screen staying valid C#, and its absence would make the display lie rather than
        // merely hold less than was typed.
        if (sanitized != value) {
            SetHint(owner, PlaygroundStrings.T(LocaleState.Current, "limit.quotes"));

            return printable;
        }

        if (printable != sanitized) {
            SetHint(owner, PlaygroundStrings.T(LocaleState.Current, "limit.printable"));

            return printable;
        }

        ClearHint(owner);

        return printable;
    }

    private Task SetMinText(string value) {
        _min = ParseAndClamp(value, "min", out _minText);
        ReconcileBoundsHint();

        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    private Task SetMaxText(string value) {
        _max = ParseAndClamp(value, "max", out _maxText);
        ReconcileBoundsHint();

        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    /// <summary>
    ///     Called after either bound's own setter runs, unconditionally. <see cref="_capHintOwner" />
    ///     names one field, but two — <see cref="_min" /> and <see cref="_max" /> — can
    ///     independently need an explanation at once, which never arose for the single length
    ///     field this replaced.
    ///
    ///     NOT JUST "WHEN THE HINT WENT AWAY". A first version returned early whenever a
    ///     setter's own call left a hint standing, on the reasoning that a field's own
    ///     complaint should not be overwritten. That missed the sibling case: typing a value
    ///     past 64 into one bound sets *that* bound's own cap message and leaves it as the
    ///     current owner, and if the *other* bound is still empty, Generate stays disabled
    ///     with a hint that only explains the field which is no longer the problem.
    ///
    ///     So this always defers to whichever bound is still <c>null</c>, even over a
    ///     legitimate cap notice for the bound just typed into. That is a real trade-off, not
    ///     a free improvement — the capped field's own "you hit 64" sentence can be replaced
    ///     by the sibling's "enter a length" — but it is the smaller loss: a capped value has
    ///     its own visible cue (the field's contents visibly snapped to 64), where a disabled
    ///     Generate with an empty required field has no cue at all beyond this hint.
    /// </summary>
    private void ReconcileBoundsHint() {
        if (_min is null) {
            SetHint("min", PlaygroundStrings.T(LocaleState.Current, "hero.lengthRequired"));
        } else if (_max is null) {
            SetHint("max", PlaygroundStrings.T(LocaleState.Current, "hero.lengthRequired"));
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    ///     Shared by <see cref="SetMinText" /> and <see cref="SetMaxText" />: bound to the raw
    ///     text rather than to <c>int</c> directly.
    ///
    ///     A first version bound <c>int</c>, and Blazor's numeric binding only calls the setter
    ///     when the raw text parses as one: a pasted value outside <see cref="int" />'s range, or
    ///     valid <c>&lt;input type="number"&gt;</c> syntax an <c>int</c> cannot represent —
    ///     <c>1e3</c>, a decimal — never reached this method at all, so neither the cap nor its
    ///     explanation ever fired for exactly the values a plain keystroke cap could not catch
    ///     either. <see cref="double" /> parses everything the input's own syntax allows, so
    ///     there is somewhere left to enforce the limit against.
    ///
    ///     Every keystroke past 64 is clamped immediately, and the first one that goes too far
    ///     sets <see cref="_capHint" /> instead of silently substituting a value the visitor
    ///     never asked for. Typing a third digit past "64" changes nothing visible after that —
    ///     the value is already 64, and re-assigning 64 does not move the caret past it — which
    ///     is the whole of what "cannot type past the limit" means for a plain
    ///     <c>&lt;input type="number"&gt;</c>: its own `max` attribute only affects the spinner
    ///     arrows, never the keyboard.
    ///
    ///     Deliberately does not compare <paramref name="value" />'s two fields against each
    ///     other — a minimum typed above the current maximum is not this playground's rule to
    ///     enforce, it is the library's own refusal at Generate (§9.9), and clamping it away
    ///     here would be exactly the neutralisation that section forbids.
    /// </summary>
    private int? ParseAndClamp(string value, string owner, out string text) {
        if (!double.TryParse(value, NumberStyles.Float, CultureInfo.InvariantCulture, out double parsed)) {
            // Not parseable at all — an empty field mid-edit, most often. The raw text is left
            // exactly as typed rather than fought, but the bound Generate actually runs with is
            // invalidated rather than left at its last-valid number, and the button is disabled
            // for the same reason (see _min's own comment).
            text = value;
            SetHint(owner, PlaygroundStrings.T(LocaleState.Current, "hero.lengthRequired"));

            return null;
        }

        // Compared before any rounding, not after: truncating "64.5" to 64 first would put it
        // on the *legal* side of the cap check, when the value actually typed was over it. The
        // cap has to see what was typed, not what it would become after this method already
        // decided to keep it.
        if (parsed > MaxLength) {
            // The clamped value is what gets displayed back, not the raw text — otherwise a
            // paste or a third digit would still *show* past 64 even though the bound itself
            // stayed capped, which is the visible half of "cannot type past the limit" this
            // method exists to hold onto.
            SetHint(owner, PlaygroundStrings.T(LocaleState.Current, "limit.length", MaxLength));
            text = MaxLength.ToString(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture);

            return MaxLength;
        }

        ClearHint(owner);

        // A value in bounds is not necessarily one WithLengthBetween(...) can take as typed —
        // it wants an int, and "12.5" is not one. Normalized to what actually runs, the same
        // reasoning as the branch above: a box reading "12.5" while Draw() quietly ran against
        // 12 would agree with nothing the visitor can see.
        int clamped = (int)Math.Clamp(Math.Truncate(parsed), 0, MaxLength);
        text = clamped.ToString(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture);

        return clamped;
    }

    private void SetHint(string owner, string message) {
        _capHint      = message;
        _capHintOwner = owner;
    }

    /// <summary>
    ///     Only clears <see cref="_capHint" /> when <paramref name="owner" /> is the field that
    ///     set it — an edit to one field must not erase a still-true explanation of why
    ///     another one changed.
    /// </summary>
    private void ClearHint(string owner) {
        if (_capHintOwner != owner) {
            return;
        }

        _capHint      = null;
        _capHintOwner = null;
    }

    private static string ReadLibraryVersion() {
        Assembly assembly = typeof(Any).Assembly;
        string version = assembly.GetCustomAttribute<AssemblyInformationalVersionAttribute>()?.InformationalVersion
                         ?? assembly.GetName().Version?.ToString()
                         ?? "unknown";

        int metadata = version.IndexOf('+');

        return metadata < 0 ? version : version.Substring(0, metadata);
    }

}
